'use strict';
// PINNED BYPASS REGRESSIONS (git-guard heredoc scanning).
//
// An exemption was attempted that stopped the "`git push` has an argument
// produced by a command substitution" check from reading the body of a quoted
// heredoc fed to python/node/cat/tee. Three rounds of adversarial review
// (~500 payloads) kept finding real bypasses of every variant: backtick-wrapped
// heredocs, backslash continuations on the opener, `{ ... } | bash` and
// `( ... ) | sh` groupings, ruby/perl/php backtick bodies, python
// commands.getoutput, multi-line variable assembly, backtick/`$(` inside
// os.system / execSync strings, backslash-newline and adjacent string literals
// inside a Python string, a `push ... $(` line without `git`, and cat/tee
// sinks writing scripts (x.sh, ~/.bashrc, .git/hooks/pre-push). A static scanner
// cannot tell prose from shell-exec strings inside an interpreter body, so the
// guard stays fail-closed: heredoc bodies are scanned as shell.
//
// Every payload below is BLOCKED by the guard today. ANY future loosening of
// heredoc scanning must keep all of them blocked. (Payloads use $(cat .f) / backticks, never a
// literal --force, so the substitution heuristic itself is what is pinned.)
// The workaround for legitimate prose is the Write tool (see docs/GUIDE.md).
// GP/SUB/BT below are placeholders for "git push", "$(" and a backtick.

